This version puts every character into a stack. Reading the stack back gives the characters in reverse order, which can then be compared with the original string.

The stack uses extra memory, but the steps are easy to follow. A null input is treated as not valid here.

```csharp
class Solution {
    public bool palindromeCheck(string s) {
        if (s == null) {
            return false;
        }

        var characters = new System.Collections.Generic.Stack<char>();
        foreach (char character in s) {
            characters.Push(character);
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < s.Length; i++) {
            if (s[i] != characters.Pop()) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the stack.

Here the string is converted to runes so normal Unicode characters are handled as single items. One slice acts like a stack and another acts like a queue. Popping from the first and reading from the second gives opposite directions.

For `abba`, the stack side reads `a`, `b`, `b`, `a`, while the queue side reads the same values. Every comparison matches, so the function returns `true`.

```go
func palindromeCheck(s string) bool {
    characters := []rune(s)
    stack := make([]rune, len(characters))
    copy(stack, characters)

    for i := 0; i < len(characters); i++ {
        stack[len(stack)-1-i] = characters[i]
    }

    for i := 0; i < len(characters); i++ {
        if characters[i] != stack[i] {
            return false
        }
    }

    return true
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` where `n` is the number of runes.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the rune slices. The original string is not modified.

Checking characters from both ends keeps the code small. If any pair is different, the string is not a palindrome. The two indexes move toward the center after every successful comparison.

For example, with `racecar`, the pairs are `r/r`, `a/a`, and `c/c`, so the method returns `true`.

```cpp
#include <string>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    bool palindromeCheck(string& s) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = static_cast<int>(s.size()) - 1;

        while (left < right) {
            if (s[left] != s[right]) {
                return false;
            }
            ++left;
            --right;
        }

        return true;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`. The input string is only read, not changed.

The recursive method compares the first and last characters, then checks the smaller inside part. Reaching the middle means every pair matched.

A mismatch stops the recursion immediately. For `level`, the checks are `l/e`? No, the actual pairs are `l/l`, `e/e`, and then the middle is reached, so the result is `true`.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean palindromeCheck(String s) {
        if (s == null) {
            return false;
        }
        return isPalindrome(s, 0, s.length() - 1);
    }

    private boolean isPalindrome(String s, int left, int right) {
        if (left >= right) {
            return true;
        }
        if (s.charAt(left) != s.charAt(right)) {
            return false;
        }
        return isPalindrome(s, left + 1, right - 1);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursive call stack. The empty string and one-character string are handled by the base case.

